WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.220
To explore the origins of The Ferryman trapped
inside of the Warren’s room of cursed artifacts,

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:08.330
we can look to the case files that the paranormal
couple has documented on each of their encounters

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:09.480
with the supernatural.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.860
This one is titled “The Ferryman” with
the words “Charon’s Coins” written below,

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:14.070
above a case number.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.970
This is the only direct reference to the entity’s
true name: Charon, which comes from Greek

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:18.970
mythology.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:22.410
He represents the first example of Greek mythos
in the Conjuring franchise, which is known

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:26.590
to explore Middle-Eastern, American and even
Mexican-Central American mythos.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:30.539
Charon is the son of Erebus (air-a-biss),
deity of darkness, and Nyx, the Goddess of

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:31.539
night.

00:00:31.539 --> 00:00:35.620
According to worldhistory.org, the first known
depiction of Charon dates back to 500 BCE

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:39.940
on ancient Greek pottery, so I will assume
that he was born sometime around then.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.711
The name Charon has been translated by several
sources to “fierce brightness”, which

00:00:43.711 --> 00:00:46.570
is certainly interesting as the offspring
of night and darkness.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:50.860
It may in fact be a euphemism for death, with
Charon potentially being named after the bright

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:55.000
light that many people report seeing before
death or during a near death experience.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.149
Charon is an example of psychopomp, a deity
responsible for transporting the souls of

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:00.690
the dead into the afterlife.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:04.299
His role was to charter souls across a body
of water, depending on which legend you’re

00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:08.500
reading, a river or lake, to drop them off
in the underworld realm of Hades:, a damp,

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.840
gloomy and lifeless domain where all mortals
are sent upon death.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.220
Referred to as the “Realm of the Dead”,
it is said that very few mortals could ever

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.860
leave Hades once they entered.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:21.160
It became tradition for humans to place coins
in the mouth of, or in some cases, like the

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:25.111
depiction seen in Annabelle Comes Home, over
the eyes of the dead, in order to pay the

00:01:25.111 --> 00:01:26.740
toll to cross the river.

00:01:27.932 --> 00:01:32.643
– [Girl's Voice] If you don't pay his toll,
he'll take your soul.

00:01:32.643 --> 00:01:33.747
(tape rewind)

00:01:33.747 --> 00:01:36.508
If you don't pay his toll,
he'll take your soul.

00:01:36.508 --> 00:01:40.400
— [Zac] Those who paid the fee would be loaded into
Charon’s Ferry and taken across to the realm

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.400
of Hades.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.530
Thus explaining where he got his nickname:
The Ferryman.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:47.340
If a mortal did not pay the fee, it is said
that their soul would be left to wander the

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.270
shores for 100 years before being allowed
to cross.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:53.960
In some stories, Charon would take and keep
the soul, while in others, they would haunt

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.290
the world as ghosts.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:59.350
To learn the full history of The Ferryman,
including details of his story from ancient

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:03.490
Greek texts, how demonologists Ed and Lorraine
Warren captured this entity in the 1900s,

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:07.511
and his resurgence in tandem with the demon
Malthus in the 1970s, stick around to the

00:02:07.511 --> 00:02:09.361
end of this video.

00:02:09.361 --> 00:02:10.944
This video is sponsored by Audible.

00:02:10.944 --> 00:02:15.758
(metal music)

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.059
Welcome to another episode of Horror History.

00:02:19.059 --> 00:02:23.209
Today, I continue my series on the villains
of the Conjurverse, albeit, with much shorter

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:27.579
hair because some of those videos were filmed
during the no-haircut era of the pandemic.

00:02:27.579 --> 00:02:30.540
You couldn’t even call me “Shaggy” in
this, it’s way longer than his.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.459
The Ferryman we are talking about today is
the Conjuring universe version seen in the

00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:38.079
2019 film Annabelle Comes Home mixed with
some of the mythos that he is based on in

00:02:38.079 --> 00:02:39.079
that movie.

00:02:39.079 --> 00:02:42.400
Charon has appeared many other times in popular
culture though, from being a boss fight in

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.609
God of War: Chains of Olympus, to being a
silent rower in Disney’s Hercules and even

00:02:46.609 --> 00:02:49.819
getting his own movie in the 2007 film: The
Ferryman.

00:02:49.819 --> 00:02:53.919
To fully understand The Ferryman’s journey,
let’s take it back about 2500 years into

00:02:53.919 --> 00:02:59.353
the stories of Euripides, Aeschylus, 
and many other tragedians of Ancient Greece.

00:02:59.353 --> 00:03:03.312
(harp music)

00:03:03.719 --> 00:03:07.480
As I mentioned, the earliest Charon depiction
was on a vase, where he appears as a bearded

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.010
man carrying a punt pole.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:12.409
These poles would be used to move boats in
shallow water by the user planting one end

00:03:12.409 --> 00:03:15.150
of the pole at the bottom of the river and
using it to propel forward.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:18.590
The most common channel cited as the river
that Charon spends his time on would be the

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:19.590
River Styx.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:24.359
If only Charon had operated a sailboat as he does in some later depictions, it
would have been the perfect setup for some

00:03:24.359 --> 00:03:25.449
kind of joke!

00:03:25.449 --> 00:03:29.379
The coins left for The Ferryman were known
as Charon’s obol, based on the ancient Greek

00:03:29.379 --> 00:03:30.379
coin, the obol.

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:33.909
It equates to about $16 or $17 USD in 2022.

00:03:33.909 --> 00:03:37.230
Different cultures have adapted the story
to fit their own coins, as we see in Annabelle

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.650
Comes Home, where Daniela comes across a pouch
of coins of various types.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:44.269
But don’t try to pay The Ferryman using
your debit card or bitcoin wallet.

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:48.099
He has no use for monetary value; what he’s
interested in is the silver.

00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:51.000
The obol was perfect for him, because the
coins were worth their weight in silver. The

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.290
obol was also used as a measurement of weight.

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:56.790
Although most of the ancient Greek tragedies
depicted the version of Charon who accepts

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.931
payments as coins placed in the corpse's mouth,
director Gary Dauberman was inspired by the

00:04:00.931 --> 00:04:04.400
images of coins placed over the victims’
eyes that he’d seen on the internet.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.769
Just as the mythos evolved to include other
coins, the description of Charon has also

00:04:07.769 --> 00:04:09.150
changed over the years.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:14.249
In 438 BCE, in Euripides play Alcestis, Charon
has evolved to use an oar, because the body

00:04:14.249 --> 00:04:17.820
of water being crossed is now an “enormous
lake of fathomless depth”.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:22.230
In 19 BCE, Virgil’s Aeneid
describes him as “appalling filthy… with

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:27.030
a bush of unkempt white beard upon his chin,
with eyes like jets of fire; and a dirty cloak

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:29.580
(which) draggles down, knotted about his shoulders.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:34.520
Around 100 CE, Seneca’s Hercules details
an old man “clad in foul garb” which is

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.280
described as an “abhorrent sight.”

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.300
His beard is unkempt and his cheeks are haggard
and sunken in.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.210
Roman poetry of the 2nd century paints an
even grimmer picture.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.710
Apuleius’s 1566 novel
The Golden Ass… shame we didn’t cover

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:50.000
that one in middle school language arts…
depicts an old dead man with decaying hands

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.000
and arms.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.191
A derivative version of the character can
also be found in Etruscan mythology, where

00:04:54.191 --> 00:04:57.250
he is known as Charun, a demon of death armed
with a hammer.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:01.030
There seems to be a gradual transformation
as we move closer to present day towards a

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:04.780
more Grim Reaper like appearance, which was
consciously made a part of his design in his

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.289
Conjurverse incarnation.

00:05:06.289 --> 00:05:09.729
Many of the tales seem to be about heroes
who crossed the river to retrieve someone

00:05:09.729 --> 00:05:11.020
or complete a challenge.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:14.969
In some mythos, The Ferryman will help the
hero, in others he needs to be convinced.

00:05:14.969 --> 00:05:18.650
In Hercules, the titular hero was tasked to
retrieve the three-headed dog who guarded

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:22.729
the gates of Hades, and The Ferryman was punished
severely for escorting a living being into

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:23.729
the realm of the dead.

00:05:23.729 --> 00:05:27.330
Pinpointing when The Warrens had their encounter
with The Ferryman is a little bit tricky.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:31.120
They keep a file in their office on the case,
and it contains clues that we can use to further

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.229
determine the history of this mysterious river
captain of the underworld.

00:05:39.308 --> 00:05:43.099
I am about to dig into one of the many case
files of the demonologists Ed and Lorraine

00:05:43.099 --> 00:05:44.099
Warren.

00:05:44.099 --> 00:05:47.210
This one is probably movie-only, but they
do have some stories that they’ve published,

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:48.939
meaning you can dig into them yourself.

00:05:48.939 --> 00:05:53.050
They explore paranormal phenomena like haunted
houses, graveyards and even a werewolf.

